A refining module may no longer change what a name in the code it inherits refers to. The inherited code is resolved again in the refining module's scope, so a declaration that the refining module added could capture a name that the base used: a function or type of the same name as one from an opened import, a nested module, a class member hiding a module-level constant, a member of an added parent trait, a local variable of a statement skeleton, or a constant or datatype constructor turning a pattern variable into a pattern, including one of a module bound to an abstract import. The inherited proofs, made about the old meaning, then held of the new one, and the refining module could prove `false`. Every name in inherited code must now resolve to the counterpart of what it resolved to in the base, and a capture is a resolution error that names the captured name and both declarations: `refining module 'B' captures the name 'F' used in code inherited from module 'A': in 'A' it refers to function 'L.F' (...), but in 'B' it would refer to function 'B.F' (...)`. To keep both declarations, rename the one in the refining module. The hidden option `--legacy-inherited-name-capture` restores the old, unsound rule, for comparison and migration only.
